---
name: curried-def-clauses
description: "a curried def lowers at its TOTAL arity and its call site flattens; a def returning a function stays nested"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `def f(a)(b)` — two clauses, one arity

`BUGS.md` `v2-front-curried-def-second-clause`. The F front dropped the second parameter clause of a
curried `def`, so the body's reference to it became a free global and F declined the whole file:

```text
def ap(n: Int)(f: Int => Int): Int = f(n)
  F emitted:  (def ap (lam 1 (app (global f) (local 0))))
  F reported: GAP — unbound global: (global f) is neither a top-level def nor an @-cell
```

Output was correct the whole time, because the fallback front compiled the file. That is what made
it worth fixing and hard to see: **any corpus case using a curried def was measuring the legacy
front while appearing to measure F.**

**This fix is two halves, and half 1 alone is a regression** — the entry records a previous attempt
that was reverted for exactly that reason:

1. the **def** must lower at the TOTAL arity, `(lam 2 …)`;
2. the **call site** must FLATTEN — `(app (global ap) 3 <lam>)`, not `(app (app (global ap) 3) <lam>)`.
   This runtime has no partial application, so with only half 1 the file goes from *"F declines,
   fallback prints 6"* to *"F accepts, program dies with `arity: 2 expected, 1 given`"*, and the
   front report flips `GAP` → `F`, which reads as progress.

```scalascript
def ap(n: Int)(f: Int => Int): Int = f(n)
def add(a: Int)(b: Int): Int = a + b
println(ap(3)((i) => i * 2))
println(add(4)(5))

// THE DISCRIMINATOR. `mk` is not curried — it is a one-clause def that RETURNS a function — and it
// is called with the same `f(a)(b)` syntax. Flattening it would produce a 2-arity call against a
// 1-arity lambda. So the fix keys on a table of curried-def NAMES, not on the shape of the call,
// and this row is what proves it.
def mk(n: Int): Int => Int = (x) => x + n
println(mk(10)(5))

// A plain multi-parameter def must be untouched by any of it.
def plain(a: Int, b: Int): Int = a + b
println(plain(1, 2))

// The second clause is a real binder, not a name that happens to resolve: shadow it.
def sh(x: Int)(x2: Int): Int = x2 - x
println(sh(10)(3))
```

## The TRAILING forms of that second clause

Half 2 above flattened the parenthesised call site and only that one. Both TRAILING spellings of
the same second clause — `f(a) { … }` and Scala 3's `f(a): p => …` — go through `blockArgApp`,
which nested unconditionally, so a curried callee was applied with one argument of two and died
with the very `arity: 2 expected, 1 given` half 2 exists to prevent. It reached the nightly:
`fewer-braces-colon` went red on the 2026-08-05 corpus contract and stayed the last regression row
in it. That case covers the COLON spelling on a non-curried callee, so neither it nor this gate
could see the pair — the rows below are the missing intersection, curried × each trailing form.

```scalascript
def apT(n: Int)(f: Int => Int): Int = f(n)

// `apT`, not `ap`: the jvm backend puts every fence of a file in ONE class, and a second
// `ap(n: Int)(f: Int => Int)` erases to the same signature — "Conflicting definitions … have the
// same type after erasure". The int and v2 lanes accept it, so this only surfaces on jvm.
// the trailing BRACE on a curried callee
def useBrace(): Int = apT(3) { (i) =>
  i * 2
}
println(useBrace())

// the trailing COLON on a curried callee — Scala 3 fewer-braces
def useColon(): Int =
  apT(3): (i) =>
    i * 2
println(useColon())

// THE DISCRIMINATOR AGAIN, for the trailing forms. `mkg` returns a function that takes a lambda,
// so `mkg(3) { … }` must stay NESTED. If the trailing-form flattening keyed on the call shape
// instead of the curried-name table, this row would die with an arity error — which is how the
// first version of half 2 was wrong, and the reason it is repeated here rather than assumed.
def mkg(n: Int): (Int => Int) => Int = (g) => g(n)
def viaBrace(): Int = mkg(3) { (i) =>
  i * 2
}
def viaColon(): Int =
  mkg(3): (i) =>
    i + 1
println(viaBrace())
println(viaColon())
```

**Three clauses are deliberately absent.** `def tri(a: Int)(b: Int)(c: Int)` dies with
`TYPEERR: cannot unify Tuple with non-Tuple` — and it does so on the *unmodified* toolchain too, so
it is a pre-existing type-checker defect rather than a front gap. Filed separately as
`v2-three-parameter-clauses-fail-typecheck` instead of being smuggled in here as a known-red row.

Which front compiled the file is pinned separately by `tests/e2e/v2-front-coverage.sh` — an output
comparison cannot see it, which is the whole reason this bug survived: every output test was green.
